# Direct attained-\(T_2+T_3\) chart: semantic substitution and bounded decision

## Disposition

The compressor remains proved. In Moh's formal-variable order
\((f,g)=(G,F)\), attained \(T_2\) lowers the possible degree of a nonzero
\(j=J(F,G)\) to 20 for \((99,66)\) and 25 for \((108,72)\). Attained
\(T_3\), with Proposition 3.2's exact derivative degree, then forces such a
nonzero \(j\) to be a scalar. The charged Theorem B excludes \(j=0\) on the
actual source faces with attained \(T_2\). Thus a compatible canonical
attained-\(T_2+T_3\) datum has \(j\in k^\times\). This is a theorem, not a
normalization: the direct rings have no \(j\) variable, \(j\)-localizer, or
raw Jacobian-tail rows, and no gauge is spent setting \(j=1\).

The earlier circuit presentations contained 7,136 acyclic arithmetic
variables for either 99 chart and 17,308 for D108. Recursive exact
substitution gives direct semantic ideals in 449, 447, and 507 variables,
with mechanically derived final counts 2,754, 2,754, and 3,196 nonzero
polynomial generators. No constraint variable is pivoted; the full 2,754-row
delta-2 stream did not finish, so no full-artifact SHA is claimed below.

The finite outcomes are:

| chart | decision | scope |
|---|---|---|
| \((99,66),\delta=2\) | **PENDING_FINAL_RESULT** | pending final custody |
| \((99,66),\delta=5/2\) | **OPEN — NOT REACHED** | delta 2 had priority |
| \(D=108\), repaired free mean | **OPEN — NOT REACHED** | delta 2 had priority |

A modular unit is only a signal. It becomes a characteristic-zero kill only
after exact-\(\mathbb Q\) confirmation on identical generators or an exact
cofactor identity. A nonunit result for a strict generator subset is
inconclusive for the full ideal. If a complete exact-\(\mathbb Q\) direct
ideal were proper, its permitted positive type would be:

> **PROPER — EXISTENTIAL NONDEGENERATE NECESSARY-CHART SURVIVOR OVER
> \(\overline{\mathbb Q}\); NOT A PAIR, NOT A CANONICAL LIFT.**

Such a survivor would not be a Keller pair and would not provide the missing
triangular lift to the canonical approximate-root recursion.

